The O3DE lighting story's cue groups
====================================

What the storyboard (`storyboard.lam`) says again and again, said once: the package `o3de_lighting_story`, which
the storyboard imports (`$$[Import* o3de_lighting_story]`; tools/video/build.py gives it to the reader). Each
group is a list of the vocabulary's attributes; a cue joins it with its own (`more`), or with `append`.

$[Import* std]$[Import* content]$[Import* present]$[Import* render_lab]$[Import* o3de_lighting]

A beat's opening keys: its demonstration and panel, the callout and the insets cleared, and how far the camera
drifts while it holds; `more` are the beat's own.

$[# {opening demo panel drift more} {append
    {' demo {callout ""} {inset ""} panel {cameraDrift drift}}
    more}]

The room's lighting as most beats show it: the sun at forty thousand lux from yaw 40 and pitch 52, the sky and its
image-based light at exposure 2, and the camera's exposure.

$[# baseLight {'
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 2.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.18}
    {sunYaw 40.0}
    {sunPitch 52.0}}]

The point low on the teapot that chapters 3 to 9 follow through the frame: its position and its normal.

$[# teapotPoint {'
    {inspectX -0.315}
    {inspectY 0.124}
    {inspectZ -1.478}
    {inspectNX -0.23}
    {inspectNY -0.02}
    {inspectNZ 0.97}}]

A target of the frame's chain in an inset: the inset's name, the target's index, and its corner; `more` sets the
rest (its title's `imageTitle` written at the cue, where the text check finds its key).

$[# {chainTarget name target corner more} {append
    {' {inset name} imageFromChainTarget {imageTarget target} corner}
    more}]

A key a moment into the sentence, before its first words.

$[# settle {cue "key" {' {delay 0.4}}}]

Camera paths several beats share (`cameraPath`): close around the sphere for the BRDF's terms, around the
material spheres, and behind the subject that light passes through.

$[# brdfSweep {' {' 0.0 6.0 3.1} {' -10.0 4.0 3.3} {' 6.0 8.0 3.0}}]
$[# surfaceSweep {' {' 4.0 8.0 3.1} {' -8.0 6.0 3.3} {' 6.0 10.0 3.0}}]
$[# transmissionSweep {' {' 35.0 6.0 1.8} {' 48.0 4.0 2.0} {' 38.0 8.0 1.7}}]

Surface points the inspected point moves to, so every view of a screen-sized resource rings the pixel a sentence
reads, and the readout reads it: each a position and its normal (the scene's objects in `scene::ids` order).

$[# atFloor [% {' 0.4 0.0 1.0 0.0 1.0 0.0}]]
$[# atBackWall [% {' 0.3 1.7 -3.0 0.0 0.0 1.0}]]
$[# atLeftWall [% {' -3.0 1.3 -0.6 1.0 0.0 0.0}]]
$[# atRightWall [% {' 3.0 1.3 -0.6 -1.0 0.0 0.0}]]
$[# atSphere [% {' -0.864 0.744 0.027 0.30 0.20 0.93}]]
$[# atBoxTop [% {' 1.05 0.9 0.15 0.0 1.0 0.0}]]
$[# atP [% {' -0.315 0.124 -1.478 -0.23 -0.02 0.97}]]
$[# objFloor 0.0]
$[# objBackWall 1.0]
$[# objLeftWall 2.0]
$[# objRightWall 3.0]
$[# objSphere 5.0]
$[# objBox 6.0]
$[# objTeapot 8.0]

The inspected point glides to `where` over a second and a half, on the object `obj`, ringed in every view.

$[# {pickTo where obj} {'
    {motion {'
        {path
            {' inspectX inspectY inspectZ inspectNX inspectNY inspectNZ}
            {' where}
            {seconds 1.5}}}}
    {inspectObject obj}
    {pickShown 1.0}}]

The readout's first `n` rows, revealed as the sentence names them.

$[# {rowsTo n} {' {readoutRows n} {blend 0.5}}]
